AWS-001 — Purpose & Scope

Document ID: AWS-001
Title: Purpose & Scope
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification establishes the Atlas Workflow Specification (AWS) as the normative standard governing workflow definition, execution, orchestration, monitoring, governance, and optimization within the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

AWS transforms engineering intent into governed operational execution by defining a canonical workflow model that is deterministic, explainable, interoperable, and implementation-independent.

2. Objectives

AWS SHALL:

Standardize workflow representation.
Define workflow semantics.
Enable governed execution.
Support deterministic orchestration.
Preserve engineering intent.
Enable interoperability.
Support automation.
Enable continuous optimization.
3. Scope

AWS governs workflows related to:

Engineering design
Review processes
Validation
Approvals
Construction execution
Project management
Digital Twins
Asset lifecycle
AI-assisted engineering
Collaborative engineering
Operational maintenance

AWS does not define:

Programming languages
Workflow engines
BPM implementations
Automation platforms
Execution technologies

These belong to implementation profiles.

4. Relationship to Other Specifications

AWS SHALL conform to:

ACC — Atlas Cognitive Codex
AHS — Atlas Hivemind Specification
ADS — Atlas Data Specification
APS — Atlas Policy Specification

Future Atlas specifications SHALL reference AWS when defining executable engineering behavior.

AWS SHALL become the normative execution standard for Atlas.

5. Canonical Definition

A Workflow is a governed sequence of engineering activities, decisions, events, and interactions performed to achieve a defined engineering objective while preserving traceability, governance, and engineering intent.

A workflow defines execution.

It does not define implementation technology.

6. Workflow Objectives

Every Atlas Workflow SHALL aim to:

Preserve engineering intent.
Enable repeatable execution.
Support governance.
Enable automation.
Preserve explainability.
Enable collaboration.
Support optimization.
Preserve auditability.
7. Design Principles

Every Atlas Workflow SHALL satisfy the following principles.

Explicit

Every workflow SHALL be explicitly defined.

Implicit workflows SHALL NOT exist.

Deterministic

Equivalent inputs SHALL produce equivalent workflow behavior unless adaptive execution is explicitly authorized.

Technology Independent

Workflow semantics SHALL remain independent of execution engines.

Governed

Every workflow SHALL be governed by Canonical Policy Objects.

Traceable

Workflow execution SHALL remain reconstructable.

Explainable

Every workflow transition SHALL be explainable.

Versioned

Workflow definitions SHALL evolve through governed versioning.

Composable

Workflows SHALL support modular composition.

8. Canonical Workflow Families

Atlas defines the following workflow families.

Workflow Family	Purpose
Design Workflow	Engineering design activities
Review Workflow	Technical review
Approval Workflow	Governance approvals
Validation Workflow	Quality verification
Construction Workflow	Construction execution
Inspection Workflow	Field verification
Maintenance Workflow	Asset maintenance
Compliance Workflow	Regulatory verification
AI Collaboration Workflow	Human-AI engineering collaboration
Digital Twin Workflow	Digital asset synchronization

Future workflow families SHALL extend this hierarchy.

